WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.720
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:11.360
Today we are going to see how NVIDIA’s light&nbsp;
transport algorithm can render this virtual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:19.440
amusement park that contains no less than&nbsp;
3.4 million light sources interactively.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.040
Yes. Millions of light sources.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.000
How is this even possible? Well, to&nbsp;
understand what is going on here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.560
we need to talk about two concepts.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:36.480
If we wish to create a truly photorealistic scene,&nbsp;
in computer graphics, we usually reach out to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:44.720
light transport simulation algorithm, and then,&nbsp;
this happens. Oh yes, concept number one. Noise!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:53.920
This is not photorealistic at all, not yet anyway.&nbsp;
Why is that? Well, during this process, we have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.920 --> 00:01:00.640
shoot millions and millions of light rays into&nbsp;
the scene to estimate how much light is bouncing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:08.160
around, and before we have simulated enough rays,&nbsp;
the inaccuracies in our estimations show up as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:16.960
noise in these images. This clears up over time,&nbsp;
but it may take from minutes to days for this to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:25.840
happen, even for a smaller scene. For instance,&nbsp;
this one took us 3 full weeks to finish. Weeks!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:34.000
Ouch. And in a moment, you will see that NVIDIA’s&nbsp;
technique can render even bigger scenes than this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:41.600
not in weeks, but in milliseconds.&nbsp;
Yes, really. I am not kidding.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:49.440
And for now, concept number two. Biased&nbsp;
and unbiased. There are different classes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:56.000
of light transport algorithms. Here you see an&nbsp;
unbiased technique that doesn’t cut any corners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.880
and now, look! Oh yes, this is a&nbsp;
biased technique, and as you see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:08.480
this one can give us a much cleaner&nbsp;
image in the same amount of time. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:13.200
the price to be paid for this is that&nbsp;
some corners have been cut in the process.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:19.200
And with that, now, hold on to your papers,&nbsp;
and have a look at NVIDIA’s technique.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.400
Oh yes, this was the scene with&nbsp;
the 3.4 million light sources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:31.200
and this method can really render not just an&nbsp;
image, but an animation of it interactively.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:38.240
That is absolutely incredible, and&nbsp;
it has even more to offer. Look!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:45.520
The light sources can even be dynamic, or in&nbsp;
other words, they can move around as they please,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:53.280
and the algorithm still doesn’t break a sweat.&nbsp;
Oh my goodness! That is absolutely amazing.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:03:01.280
Now, let’s see how it compares to a previous&nbsp;
technique. Oh boy. See the noise levels here? This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:07.840
is so noisy, we don’t even know what the scene&nbsp;
should exactly look like. But, now, when we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:14.640
a look at this technique, oh yes, now we know&nbsp;
for sure that there are some glossy reflections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:20.800
going on here. Even the unbiased version of the&nbsp;
new technique is significantly better in this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:28.000
However, look, if we are willing to let the&nbsp;
simulator cut some corners, this is the biased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:36.240
version, and, oh my goodness! Those difficult&nbsp;
glossy reflections are so much cleaner! I love it.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:44.480
This amusement park scene contains a total of over&nbsp;
20 million triangles, and once again, the biased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:50.640
version of the new technique makes it truly a&nbsp;
sight to behold. And, this does not take from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:57.680
minutes to days to compute, each of these images&nbsp;
were produced in a matter of milliseconds! Wow.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:05.360
But it gets better! How? Well, the more&nbsp;
detailed comparisons in the paper reveal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:13.600
that this method is 10 to a 100 times faster than&nbsp;
previous techniques, and it also maps really well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:20.800
onto our graphics cards. Okay, but what is behind&nbsp;
all this wizardry? How is this even possible?

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:27.760
Well, the magic behind all this is a smarter&nbsp;
allocation of these ray samples that we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:33.440
to shoot into the scene. For instance,&nbsp;
this technique does not forget what we did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:40.400
just a moment ago when we move the camera a&nbsp;
little and advance to the next image. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:46.960
lots of information that is otherwise thrown away&nbsp;
can now be reused as we advance the animation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:54.720
Now note that there are so many papers out there&nbsp;
on how to allocate these rays properly, this field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:00.960
is so mature, it truly is a challenge to create&nbsp;
something that is just a few percentage points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:08.000
better than previous techniques. It is very hard&nbsp;
to make even the tiniest difference. And to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:15.680
able to create something that is 10 to a 100 times&nbsp;
better in this environment? That is insanity.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:21.360
And, this proper ray allocation has&nbsp;
one more advantage. What is that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:28.480
Well, have a look at this. Imagine that you are&nbsp;
a good painter, and you are given this image.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:33.120
Now your task is to finish it.&nbsp;
Do you know what this depicts?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:42.400
Hmm…maybe. But knowing all the details of this&nbsp;
image is out of question. Now, look, we don’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:48.240
to live with these noisy images, we have denoising&nbsp;
algorithms tailored for light simulations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:55.840
This one does some serious legwork with this noisy&nbsp;
input, but even this one cannot possibly know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:03.120
exactly what is going on because there is so much&nbsp;
information missing from the noisy input. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:08.880
if you have been holding on to your papers so&nbsp;
far, now, squeeze that paper, because, look.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:16.720
This technique can produce this image in the same&nbsp;
amount of time. Now we’re talking! Now, let’s give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:25.680
it to the denoising algorithm, and…yes! We get&nbsp;
a much sharper, more detailed output. Actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:33.440
let’s compare it to the clean reference image. Oh&nbsp;
yes, this is much closer. And the fact that just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:40.960
one more paper down the line, we could go from&nbsp;
this to this absolutely blows my mind. So cool!

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:48.000
But wait, we noted that the biased version of the&nbsp;
technique cuts some corners. What is the price to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:55.920
be paid for this? What are we losing here?&nbsp;
Well, some energy. What does that mean? It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:02.880
means that the image it produces is typically&nbsp;
a little darker. There are other differences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:08.240
but usually, they are relatively minor,&nbsp;
making this an excellent tradeoff.

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:14.800
Adding it all together, this will be an excellent&nbsp;
tool in democratizing light transport algorithms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:20.960
and putting it into the hands of everyone.&nbsp;
And to think that years ago, we had this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:29.040
and now, we can get this running on a commodity&nbsp;
graphics card. I am truly out of words. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:35.120
pace of progress in computer graphics research is&nbsp;
nothing short of amazing. What a time to be alive!

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:43.360
Now, if you also got excited by this. Look here.&nbsp;
This is not an implementation of this paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:49.760
but the first author also has a nice little&nbsp;
webapp where you can create interesting 2D&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:56.320
light transport situations with lots of&nbsp;
caustics. And the results are absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:00.960
beautiful. The link is available in&nbsp;
the video description. Check it out!

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:59.440
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

